🔴 Insecure Data Handling (OWASP 2025)

📌 1. What is Insecure Data Handling?
Insecure Data Handling refers to improper collection, storage, transmission, or disposal of sensitive data, making it accessible to attackers.
👉 Simple Definition:
Failure to properly protect sensitive data at every stage of its lifecycle.

📌 2. Types of Sensitive Data
Passwords
Credit/Debit card details
Personal data (Aadhaar, PAN, phone number)
Session tokens / cookies
API keys & secrets
Health records

📌 3. Data Lifecycle (Where issues happen)
Data Collection → Input forms, APIs
Data Transmission → Network communication
Data Storage → Databases, logs
Data Processing → Application logic
Data Disposal → Deletion / archiving
⚠️ Vulnerabilities can exist in any stage.

📌 4. Common Insecure Practices
🔸 1. Storing Data in Plaintext
Passwords stored without hashing
🔸 2. Weak Encryption / No Encryption
Using outdated algorithms (MD5, SHA1)
🔸 3. Improper Key Management
Hardcoded keys in source code
🔸 4. Data Exposure in Logs
Sensitive data printed in logs
🔸 5. Unsecured Data Transmission
Sending data over HTTP instead of HTTPS
🔸 6. Overexposure via APIs
Returning more data than needed
🔸 7. Insecure Backup Storage
Backups publicly accessible
📌 5. Real-World Example

🔐 Password Storage Issue
❌ Bad Practice:
password = "user123"
✅ Secure Practice:
Use hashing algorithms like:
bcrypt
PBKDF2
scrypt
👉 Store:
hashed_password = bcrypt(password)

📌 6. Attack Scenarios
⚠️ Scenario 1: Database Leak
Attacker gets plaintext passwords → account takeover

⚠️ Scenario 2: Man-in-the-Middle Attack
Data intercepted over HTTP

⚠️ Scenario 3: Log File Exposure
Logs contain credit card numbers

⚠️ Scenario 4: API Data Leakage
/user/profile returns full sensitive info

📌 7. Why It’s Dangerous
Identity theft
Financial fraud
Legal penalties (GDPR, etc.)
Reputation damage

📌 8. Detection Methods
🔍 1. Code Review
Check for plaintext storage & hardcoded secrets

🔍 2. Network Analysis
Use tools like Wireshark
Check if data is encrypted

🔍 3. Security Testing
Intercept traffic using Burp Suite

🔍 4. Log Analysis
Ensure no sensitive data is logged

📌 9. Prevention Techniques
✅ 1. Strong Encryption
Use AES-256 for storage
Use TLS 1.2+ for transmission

✅ 2. Proper Hashing
Passwords must be:
Salted
Hashed (bcrypt, PBKDF2)

✅ 3. Secure Key Management
Store keys in:
Vaults (not code)

✅ 4. Data Minimization
Collect only necessary data

✅ 5. Secure Logging
Mask sensitive information

✅ 6. Access Control
Restrict who can access data

✅ 7. Secure Deletion
Properly erase unused data

📌 10. Best Practices Checklist ✅
🔒 Encrypt sensitive data
🔑 Never hardcode secrets
🧂 Use salting + hashing
🌐 Always use HTTPS
📉 Limit data exposure
🗑️ Delete unused data securely

📌 11. Mapping to OWASP Categories
Issue	OWASP Category
Plaintext passwords	Cryptographic Failures
Data leaks in API	Broken Access Control
HTTP transmission	Security Misconfiguration
Logs exposure	Sensitive Data Exposure

📌 12. One-Line Summary 💡
Insecure Data Handling occurs when sensitive data is not properly protected during storage, transmission, or processing, leading to unauthorized access or leaks.
